Hi Jocelyn,

That all looks really good to me for home-range estimation.

Assuming the data are irregularly sampled in time, fast=FALSE in variogram() is much slower to run, but will depict less false autocorrelation bias in the beginning of the variogram (which happens because of time interpolation). You don't have much data, so that shouldn't take very long to run and it might reveal that your data look even more IID.
By N>n, you are talking about DOF[area]>n? I think it should be okay if DOF[area] is a little different in the IID anisotropic case. The area variable here is sqrt(det(COV[location])), so while IID COV[location] would have an effective sample size of exactly n-1, after you take the determinant and square root, it can be a little different. In the isotropic case, sqrt(det(COV[location]))=VAR[location], so the relationship should be direct there. But admittedly, I haven't really though much about the anisotropic IID DOFs, and that does seem weird that it's bigger than n... I just double checked my code and it seems correct.
There might be a set of OUf parameters that are slightly better than IID but hard to get to because the likelihood function is multi-modal. I can take a look at it if you can send me a copy of that subset. (Also interested if the better variograms help here.)
Best,
Chris

